[FINAL-TEXT] All the evidence is in. Let me summarize what I found:

- **Router4 eth5** (`link_r4_r3`, 172.25.1.1/24) — state **DOWN**, dropping all 9 received packets
- **Router4 eth4** (`link_r4_r5`, 192.168.15.2/24) — state **DOWN** as well
- **Router4 kernel routing table** — only `192.168.200.0/24 dev eth0` (no remote routes learned)
- **Router3 → 172.25.1.1 ping** — 100% loss (can't reach router4's end of the link)
- **PC11 → router3** — router4 returns "Destination Net Unreachable" (no forwarding path)
- **Router3 itself** — fully healthy, eth4/eth5 UP, complete routing table including 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24

---

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies on **router4**, not router3 as the team suspected. Router3 is entirely healthy: its eth4 (link_r2_r3, 192.168.100.2/24) and eth5 (link_r4_r3, 172.25.1.2/24) are both UP, and its routing table correctly carries RIP-learned routes to both Location A prefixes (172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24) via router2.

The actual failure is that **router4's eth5** — the tap interface on the `link_r4_r3` collision domain (172.25.1.1/24) — has been administratively brought DOWN. Router4's eth4 (`link_r4_r5`, 192.168.15.2/24) is also DOWN, leaving router4 with only one active interface: eth0 (192.168.200.1/24, the Location C LAN). With both uplink interfaces down, the ripd process on router4 cannot exchange RIP v2 updates with router3 or router5. The result is that router4's forwarding table contains only the directly connected `192.168.200.0/24` — no routes to Location A (172.16.10.x, 172.16.20.x) or any other remote subnet. When PC11–14 send traffic destined for Location A, router4 (their default gateway) has no matching route and returns ICMP Destination Net Unreachable, which was confirmed directly from pc11.

Root cause: `ip link set eth5 down` (and eth4) was applied on router4, severing both its uplinks and collapsing its RIP-learned routing table.

Corrective fix: On router4, run `ip link set eth5 up && ip link set eth4 up`. This restores the adjacency with router3 over `link_r4_r3`; within one RIP update interval (≤30 s) router4 will re-learn all remote routes and Location C will regain full connectivity to Location A. No changes to router3 are needed.
=== END ===